chatgpt.text:
In a custom IvP behavior, do not casually read NAV_X / NAV_Y in onRunState() unless the behavior has explicitly declared that it needs them and checks that the InfoBuffer has valid values.

You should do three things.

1. Declare required InfoBuffer variables in the constructor

In your behavior constructor:

C++
BHV_MyBehavior::BHV_MyBehavior(IvPDomain domain) : IvPBehavior(domain)
{
  setParam("name", "my_behavior");

  addInfoVars("NAV_X, NAV_Y");
}

This tells the helm/InfoBuffer that your behavior depends on those MOOS variables.

2. Read them safely in onRunState()

Do not assume the variables exist. Use the getBufferDoubleVal() pattern with an ok flag:

C++
IvPFunction* BHV_MyBehavior::onRunState()
{
  bool ok_x = false;
  bool ok_y = false;

  double nav_x = getBufferDoubleVal("NAV_X", ok_x);
  double nav_y = getBufferDoubleVal("NAV_Y", ok_y);

  if(!ok_x || !ok_y) {
    postWMessage("Missing NAV_X or NAV_Y in InfoBuffer");
    return nullptr;
  }

  // Safe to use nav_x/nav_y here

  return nullptr;
}

The important point is that getBufferDoubleVal() can fail silently unless you check the boolean. If NAV_X or NAV_Y has not arrived yet, is misspelled, or is not being posted by the navigation process, your behavior may otherwise run with bogus/default values.

3. Debug the source of NAV_X and NAV_Y

If the behavior still warns that the variables are missing, check whether they are actually reaching the helm community:

Bash
uXMS NAV_X NAV_Y

Also inspect helm/behavior state with:

Bash
uHelmScope

and check for warnings from the behavior.

The MOOS-IvP-specific reason is that a behavior does not subscribe directly to MOOS mail like a normal MOOS app. It reads from the helm’s InfoBuffer. So the behavior must declare its InfoBuffer dependencies with addInfoVars(), and then it must check whether the requested values are actually available before using them.

claude.text:
Good catch — this is a classic MOOS-IvP bug. Here's what you should fix:

1. Declare the variables in the constructor

Register your interest so the MOOS community actually delivers updates to your app:

cpp
MyBehavior::MyBehavior() : IvPBehavior() {
    m_nav_x = 0.0;   // sensible defaults
    m_nav_y = 0.0;
}

And in onSetParam() or wherever you call RegisterVariables():

cpp
void MyBehavior::onSetParam() {
    IvPBehavior::onSetParam();
    addInfoVars("NAV_X, NAV_Y");  // tells the helm to watch for these
}

2. Read them safely in onRunState()

Use getBufferDoubleVal() with the existence-check overload so you can detect whether a value has actually arrived yet:

cpp
IvPFunction* MyBehavior::onRunState() {
    bool got_x = false, got_y = false;

    double nav_x = getBufferDoubleVal("NAV_X", got_x);
    double nav_y = getBufferDoubleVal("NAV_Y", got_y);

    if (!got_x || !got_y) {
        postWMessage("Waiting for NAV_X / NAV_Y");
        return nullptr;   // yield — don't produce an IvP function yet
    }

    // safe to use nav_x and nav_y here
    ...
}

3. Store them as member variables if you need them across calls

If other methods also need the position, cache them:

cpp
// header
double m_nav_x, m_nav_y;
bool   m_nav_valid;

// onRunState
m_nav_x     = nav_x;
m_nav_y     = nav_y;
m_nav_valid = true;

Why each piece matters

Step	Without it	With it
addInfoVars	Helm silently never delivers updates	Values flow in from the info buffer
Member-variable defaults	Garbage values if onRunState runs before the first update	Deterministic starting state
got_x / got_y check	Behavior acts on 0.0/0.0 or stale junk	Behavior idles gracefully until real data arrives

The postWMessage in the !got_x || !got_y branch is optional but good practice — it surfaces the "waiting" state in the MOOSDB log so you can diagnose startup timing issues quickly.

gemini.text:
If your behavior is reading NAV_X and NAV_Y from the blackboard in onRunState(), you are relying on implicit data dependencies. While this might "work" if another node happens to write them first, it’s a recipe for runtime crashes, silent bugs, and makes your behavior impossible to reuse.

Here is exactly what you should change to make your behavior robust, self-documenting, and safe.

1. Declare the Ports in providedPorts()

You need to explicitly tell the behavior tree framework that this node expects these keys. In BehaviorTree.CPP (assuming C++ based on the method names), this is done by overriding the static providedPorts() method.

Note: You mentioned the constructor, but in modern BehaviorTree.CPP (v3/v4), ports are officially declared in providedPorts(), which the constructor then utilizes.

C++
static BT::PortsList providedPorts()
{
    return {
        BT::InputPort<double>("NAV_X", "The target X coordinate for navigation"),
        BT::InputPort<double>("NAV_Y", "The target Y coordinate for navigation")
    };
}

2. Update your Constructor

Ensure your constructor passes the BT::NodeConfiguration properly so the framework can map these ports to the blackboard.

C++
MyCustomBehavior(const std::string& name, const BT::NodeConfiguration& config)
    : BT::ActionNodeBase(name, config) // Or StatefulActionNode, etc.
{
    // No need to manually extract them here if you want to read them dynamically in onRunState()
}

3. Safely Check and Read them in onRunState()

Never assume the data is there or valid. If a user forgets to wire the port in the XML, your code will fail. Use getInput() and check the Expected return value before proceeding.

C++
BT::NodeStatus onRunState() // or tick() depending on your base class
{
    double nav_x = 0.0;
    double nav_y = 0.0;

    // Use Expected<T> to safely check if the ports are available and valid
    auto msg_x = getInput<double>("NAV_X");
    auto msg_y = getInput<double>("NAV_Y");

    if (!msg_x || !msg_y)
    {
        // Log the specific error to save yourself hours of debugging later
        std::cout << "[Error] Missing or invalid inputs in MyCustomBehavior. "
                  << "X error: " << msg_x.error() 
                  << ", Y error: " << msg_y.error() << std::endl;

        return BT::NodeStatus::FAILURE;
    }

    // If we pass the check, extract the values safely
    nav_x = msg_x.value();
    nav_y = msg_y.value();

    // Your actual behavior logic goes here...

    return BT::NodeStatus::RUNNING;
}

Why these changes matter

XML Validation: By declaring them in providedPorts(), the framework will actually throw an error at startup if your XML tries to pass a completely wrong type or format, rather than crashing at runtime.

Reusability: Anyone looking at your class header can immediately see exactly what inputs this node requires to function.

Graceful Failure: If the upstream node that was supposed to calculate NAV_X and NAV_Y fails to write them, your node will now cleanly return FAILURE (allowing the tree to handle it via a fallback node) instead of throwing a segmentation fault or reading garbage memory.
